{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}ISDS Thematic Watcher — A doctrinal monitor for investor–State practice{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<section class="hero">
  <div class="hero-inner">
    <p class="hero-eyebrow">An automated thematic-surveillance instrument</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title">The ISDS Thematic Watcher</h1>
    <p class="hero-thesis">
      A zero-cost, weekly monitor for investor–State dispute settlement at one precise
      doctrinal intersection — where intellectual property is asserted as a protected
      investment, a regulatory or judicial measure is the disputed conduct, and the case
      turns on jurisdiction and admissibility.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="methodology.html">Read the Methodology</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="digests/index.html">Browse the Digest Archive</a>
    </div>
    <p class="hero-meta">
      A research instrument for the Benavides ISDS project · open feeds and primary
      repositories only · runs on free GitHub Actions minutes.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <div class="measure">
    <h2 class="section-head">What it watches</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The object of surveillance is not a word but a <em>relationship between three doctrines</em>.
      The theme was induced verbatim from three seed awards — <em>Philip Morris v. Australia</em>,
      <em>Eli Lilly v. Canada</em>, and <em>Bridgestone v. Panama</em> — and operationalised as the
      overlap of three doctrinal &ldquo;rings.&rdquo;
    </p>

    <div class="rings">
      <article class="ring-card">
        <div class="ring-num">Ring&nbsp;I</div>
        <h3>IP as a protected investment</h3>
        <p>Patents, trademarks and licences, copyrights, geographical indications, data
          exclusivity, and brand value asserted as a covered investment — most acute where
          the asset is informational or split across holding and operating entities.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ring-card ring-weighted">
        <div class="ring-num">Ring&nbsp;II <span class="weight-tag">weighted</span></div>
        <h3>A regulatory or judicial measure</h3>
        <p>Public-interest legislation, a domestic court judgment, or judicial conduct itself
          as the disputed conduct. A credible challenge to a court judgment reaches the
          MEDIUM band on its own.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ring-card">
        <div class="ring-num">Ring&nbsp;III</div>
        <h3>Jurisdiction &amp; admissibility</h3>
        <p>Abuse of right, treaty-shopping, foreseeability of the dispute, restructuring for
          treaty protection, and the contested definition of &ldquo;investor&rdquo; — the
          threshold on which the seed cases were decided.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="scoring-note">
      <span class="badge badge-high">HIGH&nbsp;&ge;70</span> two rings intersect
      <span class="sep">·</span>
      <span class="badge badge-med">MEDIUM&nbsp;40–69</span> one strong ring with a weaker tie, or a judicial-measure case alone
      <span class="sep">·</span>
      <span class="badge badge-low">LOW&nbsp;&lt;40</span> one weak ring or none
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band-alt">
  <div class="measure">
    <h2 class="section-head">How it works</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Each stage is defensive: a failing source or item is logged and skipped, never fatal.
      With no network the sources return empty; with no API key the classifier falls back to a
      deterministic keyword scorer — so a dry run works fully offline.
    </p>

    <div class="flow" role="img"
         aria-label="Workflow: open sources are politely fetched, deduplicated, keyword pre-scored, the top items enriched, then classified by an LLM or a keyword fallback, banded, selected above threshold, rendered into an annotated digest, and emailed plus committed to the repository.">
      <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-k">1</span><div><strong>Collect</strong><br>ICSID · UNCTAD · italaw · IISD ITN · IAReporter</div></div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-k">2</span><div><strong>Polite fetch &amp; deduplicate</strong><br>robots.txt · 3s/domain · identifying User-Agent · <code>state/seen.json</code></div></div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-k">3</span><div><strong>Keyword pre-score &amp; enrich</strong><br><code>fingerprint.yaml</code> lexicon · pull the notable line</div></div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-k">4</span><div><strong>Classify</strong><br>LLM few-shot, strict JSON &nbsp;<span class="flow-or">or</span>&nbsp; deterministic keyword fallback</div></div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-k">5</span><div><strong>Band &amp; select</strong><br>HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW · threshold 40 · top-N floor so the digest is never empty</div></div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-step flow-end"><span class="flow-k">6</span><div><strong>Render &amp; deliver</strong><br>annotated digest + dated archive · email recipients · commit to repo</div></div>
    </div>
    <p class="flow-caption">Figure 1. The collection-to-delivery pipeline. Each weekly run produces a
      dated, version-controlled archive folder under <code>digests/</code>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <div class="measure">
    <h2 class="section-head">What you receive</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Every Monday, an <strong>annotated-bibliography digest</strong>: each surfaced development
      as a citation line, a two-sentence descriptive-and-evaluative annotation, a quoted notable
      line drawn from the source, and the rings it matched. The same content is committed to the
      repository under a dated archive folder — browsable and citable.
    </p>
    <ul class="receive-list">
      <li><strong>A citation</strong> in Chicago form, with the source named and the URL supplied liberally.</li>
      <li><strong>An annotation</strong> that is both descriptive and evaluative, reflecting on which rings the development implicates and why it matters to the project.</li>
      <li><strong>A verbatim notable line</strong> from the source, so the reader can proceed directly to backsourcing.</li>
      <li><strong>A direct link to the original</strong> on every entry — sources are impossible to miss.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="digests/index.html">Open the latest digest</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="methodology.html">See how entries are scored</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
{% endblock %}
